course: dictionary
lesson: 1176
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word bronze is a good example. Read the words around it and ask: what does bronze mean here?
explain: «Michael Phelps won 28 Olympic medals, 23 gold, 3 silver and 2 bronze.» The medals are listed in order of value, so bronze must be the medal for third place. Here the clues are gold, silver and medals.
explain: «Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin; it is harder than either metal.» Now bronze is a material, a hard brown-gold metal made by mixing copper and tin. The words alloy and metal are the clues.
explain: «At the ancient Olympic Games, cheats paid fines that built bronze statues of Zeus.» Here bronze describes what the statues are made of. Try a different meaning in the sentence to check: if bronze meant third place, the sentence would make no sense.
fact: Bronze is a hard brown-gold metal made by mixing copper and tin.
fact: A bronze is a medal for third place, as in «2 bronze» medals in the Olympics.
fact: Bronze can also mean a statue or object made of bronze, and it can describe a reddish-brown colour, like a suntan.
fact: The Bronze Age began over 5000 years ago, and it is named after this metal.
fact: To check your meaning, put it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Michael Phelps won 2 bronze medals.» What does bronze mean here? | A statue of a god | A reddish-brown colour | *A medal for third place | A hard metal made from copper and tin
exercise: «Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin.» What is bronze in this sentence? | A statue of Zeus | A reddish-brown colour, like a suntan | A medal for third place | *A hard brown-gold metal
exercise: «Her skin was bronze after a summer on the beach.» What does bronze mean here? | A metal made of copper and tin | A medal for third place | *A reddish-brown colour, like a suntan | A statue made of metal
test: «The cheats' fines built bronze statues of Zeus.» What is bronze describing in this sentence? | *Statues or objects made of bronze | A medal won in a race | A brown-gold colour only | Copper and tin being mixed
test: «The Bronze Age began over 5000 years ago.» Which clue helps you know that bronze is linked to a metal? | Over | The number 5000 | *The word Age, with the alloy of copper and tin in the same text | The word began
exam: Sam says bronze can only mean a colour. Is Sam right? | *No, bronze can also mean a hard metal, a third-place medal, or an object made of bronze | Yes, bronze is only ever a colour | Yes, because bronze is always a medal | No, bronze is never a word children use
